
Part 1. What is the Linux Command Line?
The Linux Command Line is a text-based interface through which users interact with the operating system.

Instead of clicking buttons, we type commands.

For example:
mkdir project


This creates a directory named project.
pwd


This displays your current working directory.
ls


This displays files and directories.








